# Line endings are LF in the repository AND in the working copy, on every
# platform. Without the first half, what lands in a blob depends on each
# contributor's core.autocrlf, and the Linux CI runner receives whatever a
# Windows machine happened to commit. Without eol=lf, a Windows checkout
# converts to CRLF, so the files on disk stop matching the files CI reads and
# git warns on every add. Nothing here needs CRLF: there is no .bat or .cmd in
# the repository, which is the case where Windows actually cares.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Must keep LF even when checked out on Windows: a CR in a shebang line makes
# Linux fail with "bad interpreter".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Declared as binary instead of left to content detection. Git already guesses
# right for these, but a guess is not a contract, and normalizing a spreadsheet
# or a GIF corrupts the file.
*.xlsx binary
*.xls  binary
*.gif  binary
*.png  binary
*.zip  binary
